She turned and walked away from me. I stood there with a hollow feeling settling over me,and as I watched her go,I felt a violent wave of regret. I wanted to run after her and tell her how much I loved her son. How much he helped me. How much I needed him. That I wanted to spend the rest of my life with him because I loved him so much. I felt like I had betrayed him. I should have defended him. I should have told her he didn’t hurt me. He made me happier than I’d ever been in my life. I couldn’t function without him. I couldn’t breathe without him.
Movement drew my eyes on the other side of the large French doors and I saw Caleb walking with his brothers,a smile on his face. My feet began to move on their own and,like it only could when Caleb was involved,my brain didn’t think at all about those around me and

what they might think of me. I could think only of Caleb and being close to him.
